Canonical policy

1. MOGRI is the indivisible root.
2. Grammar is productive, not a closed inventory.
3. Standard English inflection and derivation are allowed when the root job survives.
4. New forms do not become new definitions of MOGRI.
5. Local specialised senses must be marked as local.
6. Historical or playful forms may remain attested without becoming required.
7. No numerical total of "all valid forms" is canonical.
8. A form is not invalid merely because it was coined after this file.
9. A form is invalid for canonical use when it cannot be recovered to the root job or when it silently replaces that job with a different concept.

